[[17592186054365 "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{14 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is divisible by }\\vphantom{p} 4 }_S \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\sqrt{3} \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is a rational number} }_T,\n$$\n\nthat is, $S \\lor T.$" 13194139533456 17592186045417] [17592186054366 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $7$ is odd or a multiple of $3.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is odd }}_P \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{7 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is a multiple of }3}_Q,\n$$\n\nthat is, $P \\lor Q.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $3$ is less than $7$. This corresponds to $R.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is odd} \\textbf{ or } \\vphantom{p} \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ a multiple of } 3}_{P \\, \\lor \\,Q}, \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \n\\underbrace{3 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is less than } 7 }_R\n$$\n\nthat is, $(P \\lor Q) \\land R.$" 13194139533456 17592186045417] [17592186054368 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is true, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{5\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{\\frac{1}{3}\\in Z}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533456 17592186045417] [17592186054371 "The number $7$ is multiplied by itself $6$ times, so we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7\\times 7}_{\\large{\\color{blue}6} \\textrm{ copies of } 7} = 7^{\\color{blue}6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $7^6.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417] [17592186054376 "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $6a$ and $-2a$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6a+1-2a &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{6a-2a}_{a \\textrm{ terms}}+ 1\t&\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms: \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\underbrace{6a-2a}+ 1 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t(6-2)a + 1 &=\\[5pt]\n\t\t4a + 1&\t\t\n\t}\n$$" 13194139534954 17592186045417] [17592186054377 "First, we group the like terms.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2.5x+2y-x+1.5y &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{2.5x-x}_{x \\textrm{ terms}}+\\underbrace{2y+1.5y}_{y \\textrm{ terms}} &\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\underbrace{2.5x-x}+\\underbrace{2y+1.5y} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t(2.5-1)x + (2+1.5)y &=\\[5pt]\n\t\t1.5x + 3.5y\n\t}\n$$" 13194139534954 17592186045417] [17592186054389 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q \\lor R$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{false}∧\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∨\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533456 17592186045417] [17592186054396 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(-3)^{3}}_{⏟}}_{-27}÷6=(-27)÷6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-27)÷6 & = \\\\ \\frac{(-27)}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27÷3}{6÷3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417] [17592186054397 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({{1-4}_{⏟}}_{-3})^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =(-3)^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{9}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =9\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\times 1}{1\\times 12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9÷3}{12÷3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417] [17592186054398 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, consider the expression inside the parenthesis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2} & = \\\\ 5÷4 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{\\frac{5}{4}}{\\overset{⏞}{5÷(-2)^{2}}})\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{5\\times 4}{4\\times 5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{\\cancel{5}\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times \\cancel{5}} & = \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417] [17592186054454 "**Step 1.** Write down the place value chart for $7.2$:\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $7$ | **.** | $2$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We determine the number of places to move.\n\nNotice that $1,000$ has a block of $3$ zeros:\n\n$$\n1,\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{\\color{blue}000}_{\\large{ {3} \\text{ zeros}}}\\!\\!\\!.\\:\n$$\n\nSo, we move all values to the left by $3$ places. Then, we place zeros in the empty cells on the right.\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **one