2y+5) & =3({(y+1)}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =3{(y+1)}^{2}+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3{(y+1)}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186184806 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3$. So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{3}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}) & =-3({(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186188002 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-y/2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-y/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y \\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-2{e}^{-y/2}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-2{e}^{-b/2}+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right].$ So, when $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$ and $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right),$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}}_{\\large f(x,y)} = \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_X(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186188015 "Since the missing number lies to the *left* of the number $5,285$, it must be *less than* this number. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0000}}} <       5,285.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our numbers in turn.\n\n- The number $485$ satisfies the condition since it has fewer digits than $5,285$.\n$\\underbrace{485}_{\\color{blue}{ {3} \\textrm{ digits}}} <       \\underbrace{5,285}_{\\color{blue}{ {4} \\textrm{ digits}}}$\n- The number $6,567$ does *not* satisfies the condition. Let's compare place values, *going from left to right*:In the *hundreds* place, we see that $6 > 5$. **thousands****hundreds****tens****ones**$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{5}$$2$$8$$5$$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{6}$$5$$6$$7$So, $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{6},567 >\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{5},285$.\n- The number $10,001$ does *not* satisfy the condition since it has more digits than $5,285$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536831 17592186045436] [17592186188216 "Any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ x \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}=x{{\\begin{bmatrix}