,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}(8-2) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [2,4]$ and $y\\in [0,1],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(\\frac{1}{6}x)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(4{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186188924 "Solving $-x+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=x.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}=x{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443] [17592186188925 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Solving $3x+2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{bmatrix}=x{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T},$ then \n\n$$\nT{v}_{1}=T{v}_{2}\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$ is a non-zero vector. This means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Null}(T)\\ne {0}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535474 17592186045443] [17592186188982 "Solving $x+y+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=-x-y.$ Solving $2x-y-w=0$ for $w,$ we get $w=2x-y.$  Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-y \\\\ -x-y\\end{bmatrix}=x{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =4-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443] [17592186192387 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 1.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.90=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.25 \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{2.4^2}{42} \\bigg)} \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  1.6 \\pm 1.1\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417] [17592186192388 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x= 21.3 + 10x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\beta_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=21.3 + 10x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 21.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.005} = 2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing $S_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(4-5)^{2}+(4.5-5)^{2}+(5.5-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0.25+0.25+1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 2.920 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.375\\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{5^2}{2.5} \\bigg)} \\approx 11.0\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  21.3 \\pm 11.0\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417] [17592186192410 "We're given that\n\n$$\ng(x) = x\\cdot f(x) > 0\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have $f'(x) \\gt 0$.\n- Statement II is false. Differentiating $g(x) = x \\cdot f(x)$, we get\n$g^{′}(x)|= (x)^{′} \\cdot f(x) + x \\cdot f^{′}(x); = f(x) + x \\cdot f^{′}(x)$.\nNow, since $f(x)$ is negative and strictly increasing over the interval $(-2,-1)$, we have\n$f(x) \\lt 0, \\qquad x \\lt 0, \\qquad f'(x) \\gt 0$.\nHence, we obtain\n$g'(x) = \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\lt 0} + \\underbrace{x}_{< 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} = \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\lt 0} + \\underbrace{x \\cdot f'(x)}_{\\lt 0} \\lt 0$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $g'(x) \\lt 0$, the function $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing. Therefore, the left Riemann sum gives an *overerestimate* of the area under the curve $y = x \\cdot f(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533376 17592186045417] [17592186192421 "Using the constant multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{100}\\dfrac14 =\\dfrac14\\cdot \\sum_{i=1}^{100} 1\n$$\n\nThen, we note that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{100} 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots + 1}_{100\\,\\textrm{times}} = 100\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14\\cdot \\sum_{i=1}^{100} 1 = \\dfrac14\\cdot 100= 25\n$$" 13194139533376 17592186045417] [17592186192490 "First, let's simplify our diagram, keeping only the relevant information:\n\n![](images/29/29502b2df474fd3da9b08d5760a3a80468dc2d366b88b7b3be5a22ce82e8d4f4.png)\n\nNow, let's recall the SAS similarity criterion:\n\n> *Two triangles are similar if the ratio of the lengths of two pairs of corresponding sides is the same and the angles included by these sides are congruent.*\n\nNotice that $\\triangle BOA$ and $\\triangle MON$ have a pair of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\angle BOA \\cong \\angle MON\n$$\n\nLet's list the sides that enclose the congruent angle for each triangle, from smallest to largest:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{({\\color{blue}{9}},{\\color{blue}{12}})}_{\\triangle BOA}, \\qquad \\underbrace{({\\color{red}{12}},{\\color{red}{16}})}_{\\triangle MON}\n$$\n\nNotice that the ratios of these lengths are in proportion:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{\\color{red}12} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{\\color{red}16} = \\dfrac34\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle BOA \\sim \\triangle MON$ by the SAS criterion." 13194139533376 17592186045417] [17592186192562 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\ncan be viewed as the sum of $9$ instances of the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} \n}^{\\large { {9} \\textrm{ times}}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nExamining the known fractions on the left-hand side of the given equation, from left to right, we have the following:\n\n- $2$ instances of $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ gives the fraction $\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}$\n- $4$ instances of $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ gives the fraction $\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}} + \\underbrace{\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}} +\n\t\\color{violet}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}  \n}^{\\large { {9} \\textrm{ times}}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nWe have $3$ instances of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1{10}\n$$\n\nremaining, and these give the fraction\n\n$$\n{\\color{violet}{\\dfrac3{10}}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} }_{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}} + \n\t\\underbrace{\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10} }_{\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}} + \n\t\\underbrace{\\color{violet}\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+ \\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\\color{violet}\\dfrac{3}{10}}\n}^{\\large { {9} \\textrm{ times}}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nThis gives the decomposition\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}} + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}}+ \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{violet}\\dfrac{3}{10}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing fraction is\n\n$$\n{\\color{violet}{\\dfrac3{10}}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436] [17592186192564 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\ncan be viewed as the sum of the whole and fractional parts:\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} = 3 + \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nIn turn, the whole number $3$ can be written as\n\n$$\n3 = 1 + 1 + 1= \\dfrac{4}{4} + \\dfrac{4}{4} + \\dfrac{4}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{4}{4} + \\dfrac{4}{4} + \\dfrac{4}{4}}_{\\large 3} + \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{4+4+4+1}{4} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}13}{4}\n$$\n\nAs a result, any decomposition of\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nmust contain fractions\n\n- whose denominator is $4$, and\n- the sum of all numerators gives ${\\color{blue}13}$.\n\nAmong the given options, the only fraction with these properties is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nIndeed, its denominator is $4$ and\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\dfrac{5}{4}} + \\dfrac{2}{4} + \\dfrac{6}{4} = \\dfrac{5+2+6}{4} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}13}{4}\n$$" 13194139533376 17592186045417] [17592186192979 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.5+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=3.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.101)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{18,0.025}=2.101.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.101\\cdot \\sqrt{8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{24})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.5\\pm 2.8\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417] [17592186193027 "We're given that $g(x)=x\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Differentiating $g(x)=x\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}\\cdot f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(1,4),$ we have \n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{x}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535509 17592186045417] [17592186198021 "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf F(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{-y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2x)-(x)\\cdot (2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+2y]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7x}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045417] [17592186198076 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y) = (0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045417] [17592186198100 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is positive and a multiple of $4.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x \\,\\textrm{is positive}}_P \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{x \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is a multiple of }4 }_M,\n$$\n\nthat is, $P \\land M.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is negative. This corresponds to $N.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x \\,\\textrm{ is positive}\\,\\textrm{and a multiple of }4 }_{P \\land M} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{x \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is negative} }_N,\n$$\n\nthat is, $(P \\land M) \\lor N.$" 13194139533457 17592186045417] [17592186198201 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nm \\textrm{is odd and} n \\textrm{is even} \\Rightarrow m - n \\textrm{is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ is odd and $n$ is even.\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is odd and $n$ is even. Then, $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the oddness of $m$ and the evenness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nm=2a+1\n$$\n\nand $n=2b$ into our expression $m - n$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Therefore, we obtain\n> $m - n|= (2a + 1) - (2b); = 2a + 1 - 2b; = (2a - 2b) + 1; = 2(a - b)_(⏟)_(c) + 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer $c=a - b$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nm - n = 2c+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $m - n$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=a - b$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore,\n> $m - n|= 2(a - b)_(⏟)_(c) + 1; = 2c + 1$\n> which is one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that $m - n$ is odd." 13194139533377 17592186045417] [17592186198213 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(m-1)n^2 \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\textrm{is even and} n \\textrm{is odd} \\right) \\Rightarrow \\neg\\left((m-1)n^2\\textrm{is odd}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\textrm{is even and} n \\textrm{is odd} \\right) \\Rightarrow (m-1)n^2 \\textrm{is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even and $n$ is odd\", we use De Morgan's law for conjunctions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n¬(mis even andnis odd) &= ¬(mis even∧nis odd) \\\\\n&= ¬(mis even)∨¬(nis odd) \\\\\n&= mis odd∨nis even \\\\\n&= mis odd ornis even\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (m-1)n^2 \\text{is even}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nNow, we have two cases: $m$ is odd, or $n$ is even.\n\n- First, we consider the case when $m$ is odd.\n\n> **Case 1**. If $m$ is odd, then $m=2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= (2a + 1 - 1)n^{2}; = 2an^{2}; = 2(an^{2})_(⏟)_(b)$.\n> Let $b=an^2$. Since $a$ and $n$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= 2(an^{2})_(⏟)_(b); = 2b$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$. Hence, $(m-1)n^2$ is even.\n\n- Now, we consider the case when $n$ is even.\n\n> **Case 2**. If $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= (m - 1)(2a)^{2}; = 4(m - 1)a^{2}; = 2(2(m - 1)a^{2})_(⏟)_(b)$.\n> Let $b = 2(m-1)a^2$. Since $a$ and $m$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= 2(2(m - 1)a^{2})_(⏟)_(b); = 2b$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$. Hence, $(m-1)n^2$ is even.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, combining the cases above, we conclude that $(m-1)n^2$ is odd only if $m$ is even and $n$ is odd." 13194139533377 17592186045417] [17592186198232 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nm \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ is even and $n$ is odd.\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is even and $n$ is odd. Then, $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the evenness of $m$ and the oddness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $m=2a$ and\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $mn$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore, we have\n> $mn|= (2a) \\cdot (2b + 1); = 4ab + 2a; = 2(2ab + a)_(⏟)_(c)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=2ab+a\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that $mn = 2c$, which shows that $mn$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=2ab+a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore,\n> $mn|= 2(2ab + a)_(⏟)_(c); = 2c$\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that $mn$ is even." 13194139533377 17592186045417] [17592186198233 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nmn \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\neg\\left(mn \\text{is odd}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn\\text{is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is odd or $n$ is odd\", we use De Morgan's law for disjunctions.\n\n$$\n¬(mis odd ornis odd) \\mid ⇔¬(mis odd∨nis odd); ⇔¬(mis odd)∧¬(nis odd); ⇔mis even∧nis even; ⇔mis even andnis even\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our original statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $m \\text{is even and} n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is even}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ and $n$ are even.\n\n> If $m$ and $n$ are even, then\n> $m = 2a \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = 2b$,\n> where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n\nWe substitute $m=2a$ and $n=2b$ into the expression $mn$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $mn|= (2a)(2b); = 4ab; = 2(2ab)_(⏟)_(c)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer $c=2ab$ (highlighted above), we have that $mn= 2c$, which shows that $mn$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=2ab$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $mn|= 2(2ab)_(⏟)_(c); = 2c$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $mn$ is even." 13194139533377 17592186045417] [17592186198594 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\,2y+(x-y),\\,xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{xy}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045417] [17592186198615 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩{|}_{z=-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨{{{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{{e}^{y}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1-x-y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({e}^{x})\\cdot (-1)-({e}^{y})\\cdot (-1)+1-x-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{x}y+{e}^{y}+y-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+e+1-x-\\frac{1}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}-x-\\frac{1}{2}+e)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+ex]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+e-1 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045417] [17592186198645 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =4{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+4\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+x+(-2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-2)+(-2)+2=-2.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045417] [17592186198655 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+(-4)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{2}+2xy+(-2){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-\\frac{1}{2})+2+(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045417] [17592186198689 "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is odd}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨Q.$" 13194139533457 17592186045417] [17592186198690 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{X}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{Y},\n$$\n\nthat is, $X∧Y.$" 13194139533466 17592186045417] [17592186198859 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and IV only.\"" 13194139534426 17592186045417] [17592186203674 "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b){:} \\quad 3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\n\n- $Q(a,b){:} \\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P \\Rightarrow Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6) \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\na \\not\\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6).\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b) =(0,2)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na = 0, \\qquad\nb = 2.\n$$\n\nThen, $3a=0$ and $3b=6.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_{3a} \\equiv \\underbrace{6}_{3b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n\\underbrace{0}_{a} \\not\\equiv \\underbrace{2}_{b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=0$ and $b=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417] [17592186203675 "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $3-\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rearrange the equation, leaving a root on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $3 - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b} - 3; \\sqrt{2}|= 3 - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b}{b} - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b - a}{b}^(⏞)^(c)$\n\nNow, if we define $c=3b-a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=3b-a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational." 13194139533378 17592186045417] [17592186203724 "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nis rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the equation to give a radical on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (2a^(⏞)^(c))/(b)$.\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=2a$. Since $2$ and $a$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational." 13194139533378 17592186045417] [17592186203854 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+7 & =-4x+5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =-4x+5+4x \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+4x+7 & =5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7-7 & =5-7 \\\\ 2nx+4x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x  = -2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x  = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+4 & =0 \\\\ 2n+4-4 & =0-4 \\\\ 2n & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{2n}{2} & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\ n & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045417] [17592186203878 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-9)z+2 & =-1 \\\\ (r-9)z+2-2 & =-1-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{(r-9)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{-3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-9 & =0 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186204214 "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(4,8)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=20$ and $5b=40.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{20}_{⏟}}_{5a}≡{{40}_{⏟}}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{4}_{⏟}}_{a}≢{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=4$ and $b=8$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417] [17592186204597 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4)x+2 & =5 \\\\ 2(p-4)x+2-2 & =5-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{2(p-4)}_{a}x & =\\underbrace{3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(p-4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(p-4)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ p-4 & =0 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186204598 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $3x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2px & =3x+5 \\\\ 2px-3x & =3x+5-3x \\\\ 2px-3x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2p-3)}_{a}x=\\underbrace{5}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2p-3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-3 & =0 \\\\ 2p-3+3 & =0+3 \\\\ 2p & =3 \\\\ \\frac{2p}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186208402 "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 49; y = x^{2} - 2x + 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 49$ into the second equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 49 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 - 49 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $-48$ and add to $-2$. These are $-8$ and $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}- 8x + 6x_(⏟)_(-2x) - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 8) + 6(x - 8) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 8)(x + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x - 8 = 0 & orx + 6 = 0 \\\\ x = 8 & orx =-6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the possible value of $x$ is $8$." 13194139533379 17592186045417] [17592186208477 "When a new data point is added, the mean of the dataset will change, and the median of the dataset may change:\n\n- The mean of the dataset moves toward that new data point.\n- The median of the dataset moves toward that new data point or stays the same.\n\nIn dataset $M$, all values are in the range $20$ and $60$, so the mean and the median are in this range.\n\n- Dataset $N$ is formed by adding the value $500$ to $M$. Since $500$ is far above every value in $M$ (all are at most $60$), the new mean for $N$ must be *greater* than the mean of $M$.So, the mean must be greater for dataset $N$ than for dataset $M$, meaning statement I is true and statement III is false.\n- To find the median, we list the dataset $M$ in ascending order:\n$M_1 \\le \\dots \\le M_{52}$\nDataset $M$ has $52$ values, so the median is the average of the $26$th and $27$th values, $\\dfrac{M_{26}+M_{27}}{2}$. On the other hand, dataset $N$ has $53$ values:\n$\\underbrace{M_1}_{N_1} \\le \\dots \\le \\underbrace{M_{52}}_{N_{52}} < \\underbrace{500}_{N_{53}}$\nSo, the median value is the $27$th value:\n$\\text{median}(N)=N_{27}=M_{27}\\ \\ \\text{and}\\ \\ \\text{median}(M)=\\frac{M_{26}+M_{27}}{2}\\le M_{27}=\\text{median}(N)$.\nThe inequality can be strict (if $M_{26} < M_{27}$) or an equality (if $M_{26}=M_{27}$).As a result, the median might not be greater for dataset $N$ than for dataset $M$, meaning statement IV is true and statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **“I and IV only.”**" 13194139533379 17592186045417] [17592186212760 "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(y - 1)^3(y + 2) + (y + 4)(y - 1)^2$.\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(y - 1)^2$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(y - 1)^2\\left[(y - 1)(y + 2) + (y + 4)\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} ( y - 1 {)}^{2} [ ( y - 1 ) ( y + 2 ) + ( y + 4 ) ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( y - 1 {)}^{2} [ {y}^{2} + y - 2 + y + 4 ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( y - 1 {)}^{2} \\cdot  \\underset{g ( y )}{\\underbrace{( {y}^{2} + 2 y + 2 )}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(y) = y^2 + 2y + 2\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436] [17592186212765 "First, we factor the denominator in the first term, $2x^2-11x+5$.\n\nTo factor this quadratic expression, we need to find two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 5=10\n$$\n\nand add to $-11$. These are $-10$ and $-1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 11x + 5 &= 2x^{2}- 10x - x_(⏟)_(-11x) + 5 \\\\\n&= (2x^{2} - 10x) + (-x + 5) \\\\\n&= 2x(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) \\\\\n&= (x - 5)(2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2x^2-11x+5}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5} = \\dfrac{5}{(x-5)(2x-1)}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can put each rational expression over a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the second fraction by $(2x-1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{7x}{x - 5} &= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{2x - 1}{2x - 1} \\cdot \\frac{7x}{x - 5} \\\\\n&= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) &= (5 + 7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (5 + 14x^{2} - 7x)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (14x^{2} - 7x + 5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045417] [17592186212835 "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(7-2a)^2$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first term in this expression contains a factor of $(2a-7)^2$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of $(7-2a)^2$.\n\nThese factors differ only by the sign:\n\n$$\n7-2a = -(2a-7)\n$$\n\nHowever, the squaring eliminates the minus sign:\n\n$$\n(7-2a)^2 = (2a-7)^2\n$$\n\nSo, we can simply rewrite the second term:\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(2a-7)^2$\n\nNow, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(2a-7)^2 \\left[(2a-7)(a+2) + (3a+4)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2a - 7)^{2}[(2a - 7)(a + 2) + (3a + 4)] &=  \\\\\n(2a - 7)^{2}[2a^{2} + 4a - 7a - 14 + 3a + 4] &=  \\\\\n&= (2a - 7)^{2}(2a^{2} - 10)_(⏟)_(g(a))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(a) = 2a^2 - 10\n$$" 13194139533380 17592186045417] [17592186216892 "When a transversal intersects two parallel lines, it creates eight angles in total: four acute angles and four obtuse angles. All acute angles are congruent, and all obtuse angles are congruent, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b463497712cb58cf8ad57e55a177ca0d9c645207348232d5875f6e6976c49c3.png)\n\nLet $\\angle A$ be an acute angle and $\\angle O$ be an obtuse angle.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = (96 - 5t)^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe are told that the sum of one obtuse angle and two acute angles is $(k-5t)^\\circ$. Writing as an equation, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle O + 2m\\angle A = (k-5t)^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\angle A$ and $\\angle O$ are supplementary. Therefore, the left-hand side of the above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠O + 2m∠A &= m∠O + m∠A_(⏟)_(180^{∘}) + m∠A \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} + (96 - 5t)^{∘} \\\\\n&= 276^{∘} - 5t^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can equate this to $(k-5t)^\\circ$ and solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n276^{∘} - 5t^{∘} &= k^{∘} - 5t^{∘} \\\\\n276 &= k \\\\\nk &= 276\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045417] [17592186216958 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The current density of a wire is defined as the **amount of electric current,** in microamperes, that passes perpendicularly through the wire’s cross-sectional surface, **divided by** the **area of that surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $J$ is the current density, in microamperes per square meter.\n- $I$ is the current, in microamperes\n- $A$ is the cross-sectional area, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that the current density is the current divided by the area:\n\n$$\nJ = \\frac{I}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{microamperes per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} J} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{microamperes}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} I/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $I = 12$ microamperes\n- $A = 0.004$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= \\frac{12microamperes}{0.004square meters} \\\\\n&= 3000microamperes per square meter\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the current density is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3000}$ microamperes per square meter." 13194139533381 17592186045417] [17592186216963 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux density on a surface is defined as the **magnetic flux**, in webers, passing perpendicularly through the surface, **divided by** the **area of the surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $B$ is the magnetic flux density, in webers per square meter.\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\nB = \\frac{\\Phi}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} B} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{webers}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $B = 10$ webers per square meter\n- $A = 7$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= \\frac{Φ}{A} \\\\\n10 &= \\frac{Φ}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we find\n\n$$\n\\Phi = 10 \\cdot 7 = 70\\,\\,\\textrm{webers}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $70$ webers." 13194139533381 17592186045417] [17592186216966 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux of a magnetic field passing perpendicularly through a surface is defined as the **product** of the **magnetic field strength**, in webers per square meter, and the **area of the surface,** measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $B$ is the magnetic field strength, in webers per square meter.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n- $\\ell$ is the length of the rectangle, in meters.\n- $w$ is the width of the rectangle, in meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\n\\Phi = B \\cdot A\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{webers per square meter})(\\textrm{square meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} B \\cdot A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nSince the width is four times the length, we have $w = 4\\ell$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\ell \\cdot w = \\ell \\cdot (4\\ell) = 4\\ell^2\n$$\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $\\ell = 1.5$ meters\n- $B = 4$ weber per square meter\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = 4 \\cdot (1.5)^2 = 9\\ \\text{square meters}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula for $\\Phi$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ &= 4 \\cdot 9 = 36webers\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $36$ webers." 13194139533381 17592186045417] [17592186216970 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The force exerted on a stretched spring is defined as the **product** of the **spring constant**, in newtons per meter, and the **extension of the spring**, in meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $F$ is the force exerted on the spring, in newtons.\n- $k$ is the spring constant, in newtons per meter.\n- $\\Delta x$ is the extension of the spring, in meters.\n- $L_0$ is the natural length of the spring, in meters.\n- $L$ is the stretched length of the spring, in meters.\n\nThe extension of the spring equals the change in length of the spring:\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = L - L_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force is related to the extension by\n\n$$\nF = k \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{newtons}}_{\\textrm{units of} F} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{newtons per meter})(\\textrm{meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} k \\cdot \\Delta x}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following information:\n\n- $F = 30$ newtons\n- $k = 120$ newtons per meter\n- $L_0 = 0.46$ meters\n\nRearranging the formula for the extension, we get\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\frac{F}{k}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔx &= \\frac{30}{120} = 0.25m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the stretched length of the spring is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= L_{0} + Δx \\\\\n&= 0.46 + 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0.71m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stretched length of the spring is $0.71$ meters." 13194139533381 17592186045417] [17592186216971 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The specific heat capacity of a substance is defined as the amount of energy, in joules $(\\textrm{J})$, required to raise the temperature of $1$ gram $(\\textrm{g})$ of the substance by $1$ degree Celsius $(^\\circ\\textrm{C})$.\n\nWe're also given the following information:\n\n> The specific heat capacity of copper is approximately $0.39 \\,\\tfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}$.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $Q$ is the energy required, in joules $(\\textrm{J})$\n- $m$ is the mass of the substance, in grams $(\\textrm{g})$\n- $\\Delta T$ is the **change** in temperature, in degrees Celsius $({}^\\circ\\textrm{C})$\n- $c$ is the specific heat capacity, in joules per gram per degree Celsius $\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}\\right)$\n\nNotice that the units of $c$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}\n$$\n\nwhich suggests that our variables are related by the following formula:\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{Q}{m\\Delta T}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}}_{\\textrm{units of} c} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}}_{\\textrm{units of} Q/m\\Delta T}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following information:\n\n- $m = 100\\,\\textrm{g}$\n- $c = 0.39 \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{J}}{\\textrm{g} \\cdot {}^\\circ \\textrm{C}}$\n- Initial temperature $= 15\\,^\\circ\\!\\textrm{C}$\n- Final temperature $= 40\\,^\\circ\\!\\textrm{C}$\n\nSo, the temperature change is\n\n$$\n\\Delta T = 40 - 15 = 25\\,^\\circ\\!\\textrm{C}\n$$\n\nSince we wish to find the energy required, $Q$, let's make $Q$ the subject in our formula. This gives\n\n$$\nQ = mc\\Delta T\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ &= 100 \\cdot 0.39 \\cdot 25 \\\\\n&= 975J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the energy required is approximately $975$ joules." 13194139533381 17592186045417] [17592186216980 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045417] [17592186217792 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}[(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$" 13194139533467 17592186045417] [17592186221091 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - 9)z + 2 &= -1 \\\\\n(r - 9)z + 2 - 2 &= -1 - 2 \\\\\n(r - 9)_(⏟)_(a)z &= -3_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n$r - 9 = 0$.\n\nSolving this for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr - 9 &= 0 \\\\\nr &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045417] [17592186221092 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b$.\n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2nx + 7 &= -4x + 5 \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= -4x + 5 + 4x \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= 5 \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 - 7 &= 5 - 7 \\\\\n2nx + 4x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x = -2\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2n + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2n + 4 - 4 &= 0 - 4 \\\\\n2n &= -4 \\\\\n\\frac{2n}{2} &= \\frac{-4}{2} \\\\\nn &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045417] [17592186224846 "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 9m + 14|= 0; m^{2}+ 7m + 2m_(⏟)_(9m) + 14|= 0; m(m + 7) + 2(m + 7)|= 0; (m + 7)(m + 2)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 7 = 0 & ⇒m =-7 \\\\ m + 2 = 0 & ⇒m =-2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$r =-7ands =-2$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 5m + 4|= 0; m^{2}+ m + 4m_(⏟)_(5m) + 4|= 0; m(m + 1) + 4(m + 1)|= 0; (m + 1)(m + 4)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 1 = 0 & ⇒m =-1 \\\\ m + 4 = 0 & ⇒m =-4\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$t =-4andu =-1$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186224892 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{z - 30}{6}\n$$\n\nand asked to rewrite it in the form\n\n$$\nz = p u + q\n$$\n\nin order to determine the value of $p$.\n\nFirst, we multiply *both sides* of the initial equation by $6$ to eliminate the fraction:\n$6u = z - 30$\n\nThen, we add *$30$ to both sides* to solve for $z{:}$\n\n$$\nz = 6u + 30\n$$\n\nFinally, we identify the coefficient of $u$. The equation is now in the form\n\n$$\nz = \\underbrace{6}_{p} \\cdot u + \\underbrace{30}_{q}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $p$ is $6$." 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186224898 "We are given a system of equations and asked to find $y_1 + y_2$, the sum of the $y$-coordinates of the two points where the graphs intersect.\n\nFirst, we substitute the expression for $y$ into the second equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 2 \\\\\nx - (x^{2} - 5x + 3) &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 3 &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $5$ and add to $-6$. These are $-1$ and $-5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x - x_(⏟)_(-6x) + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 5)(x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ using\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 5x + 3{:}\n$$\n\n- If $x_1 = 5$, then\n$y_1 = 5^2 - 5(5) + 3 = 25 - 25 + 3 = 3$\n- If $x_2 = 1$, then\n$y_2 = 1^2 - 5(1)+3=1 - 5 + 3 = -1$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\ny_1 + y_2 = 3 + (-1) = 2\n$$" 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186224993 "To factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-30$ and add to $-1$. These two numbers are $-6$ and $5$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - t - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2}- 6t + 5t_(⏟)_(-t) - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt(t - 6) + 5(t - 6) &= 0 \\\\\n(t - 6)(t + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}t - 6 = 0 & ⇒t = 6 \\\\ t + 5 = 0 & ⇒t =-5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that $k$ is a solution to our equation, with $k \\gt 0$. Therefore, we conclude that $k = 6$." 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186224994 "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-p^{2} - 3p + 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-4$ and add to $3$. These two numbers are $4$ and $-1$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2}+ 4p - p_(⏟)_(3p) - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np(p + 4) - 1(p + 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(p + 4)(p - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}p + 4 = 0 & ⇒p =-4 \\\\ p - 1 = 0 & ⇒p = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **positive** solution to the given equation is $p = 1$." 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186224995 "To factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n4 \\cdot (-15) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4y^{2} - 17y - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4y^{2}- 20y + 3y_(⏟)_(-17y) - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 5)(4y + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$." 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186225049 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The current density of a wire is defined as the **amount of electric current,** in microamperes, that passes perpendicularly through the wire’s cross-sectional surface, **divided by** the **area of that surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $J$ is the current density, in microamperes per square meter.\n- $I$ is the current, in microamperes\n- $A$ is the cross-sectional area, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that the current density is the current divided by the area:\n\n$$\nJ = \\frac{I}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{microamperes per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} J} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{microamperes}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} I/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $I = 12$ microamperes\n- $A = 0.004$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= \\frac{12microamperes}{0.004square meters} \\\\\n&= 3000microamperes per square meter\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the current density is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3000}$ microamperes per square meter." 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186225050 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux density on a surface is defined as the **magnetic flux**, in webers, passing perpendicularly through the surface, **divided by** the **area of the surface**, measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $B$ is the magnetic flux density, in webers per square meter.\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\nB = \\frac{\\Phi}{A}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of this formula:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers per square meter}}_{\\textrm{units of} B} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\textrm{webers}}{\\textrm{square meters}}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi/A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $B = 10$ webers per square meter\n- $A = 7$ square meters\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= \\frac{Φ}{A} \\\\\n10 &= \\frac{Φ}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we find\n\n$$\n\\Phi = 10 \\cdot 7 = 70\\,\\,\\textrm{webers}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $70$ webers." 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186225051 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The force exerted on a stretched spring is defined as the **product** of the **spring constant**, in newtons per meter, and the **extension of the spring**, in meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $F$ is the force exerted on the spring, in newtons.\n- $k$ is the spring constant, in newtons per meter.\n- $\\Delta x$ is the extension of the spring, in meters.\n- $L_0$ is the natural length of the spring, in meters.\n- $L$ is the stretched length of the spring, in meters.\n\nThe extension of the spring equals the change in length of the spring:\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = L - L_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force is related to the extension by\n\n$$\nF = k \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{newtons}}_{\\textrm{units of} F} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{newtons per meter})(\\textrm{meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} k \\cdot \\Delta x}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nNow, we are given the following information:\n\n- $F = 30$ newtons\n- $k = 120$ newtons per meter\n- $L_0 = 0.46$ meters\n\nRearranging the formula for the extension, we get\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\frac{F}{k}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔx &= \\frac{30}{120} = 0.25m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the stretched length of the spring is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= L_{0} + Δx \\\\\n&= 0.46 + 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0.71m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stretched length of the spring is $0.71$ meters." 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186225052 "First, let's examine the definition that's given to us:\n\n> The magnetic flux of a magnetic field passing perpendicularly through a surface is defined as the **product** of the **magnetic field strength**, in webers per square meter, and the **area of the surface,** measured in square meters.\n\nSo, let's define the following variables:\n\n- $\\Phi$ is the magnetic flux, in webers.\n- $B$ is the magnetic field strength, in webers per square meter.\n- $A$ is the area of the surface, in square meters.\n- $\\ell$ is the length of the rectangle, in meters.\n- $w$ is the width of the rectangle, in meters.\n\nFrom the definition that's given, we have that\n\n$$\n\\Phi = B \\cdot A\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{webers}}_{\\textrm{units of} \\Phi} = \\underbrace{(\\textrm{webers per square meter})(\\textrm{square meters})}_{\\textrm{units of} B \\cdot A}\n$$\n\nThe units on both sides are the same, so our formula looks correct.\n\nSince the width is four times the length, we have $w = 4\\ell$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\ell \\cdot w = \\ell \\cdot (4\\ell) = 4\\ell^2\n$$\n\nNow, we are given the following data:\n\n- $\\ell = 1.5$ meters\n- $B = 4$ weber per square meter\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = 4 \\cdot (1.5)^2 = 9\\ \\text{square meters}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula for $\\Phi$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ &= 4 \\cdot 9 = 36webers\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnetic flux is $36$ webers." 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186225062 "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12p^{2} &= -17p - 5 \\\\\n- 12p^{2} + 17p + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n12 \\cdot (-5) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2}- 20p + 3p_(⏟)_(-17p) - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n4p(3p - 5) + 1(3p - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(4p + 1)(3p - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4p + 1 = 0 & ⇒p =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 3p - 5 = 0 & ⇒p = \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" 13194139533383 17592186045417] [17592186228831 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1 \\\\  & =3{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045417] [17592186228914 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/80/8004eea851883dbf0360ca3e5f39678967cdf5060668508f65cf3e5d54d6b027.svg)\nwhere ![](images/eb/ebe84b2cfbe7f95afe2c819b1d1d7790061c81744295076ac5bc739c13832dbe.svg) and ![](images/ed/edd1f7c4e50501b6849b37c98e822621d64d2f5d90a5de35b96d569814bb2a3a.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/5c/5c91edafde3ff3c119a58783296b9f8c56ecd3604d6b663af6d764a911fe1d72.svg)\n\nGiven the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n![](images/ed/ed77716162d9ecdeee50cd26172985ed4a5038cad903ca4729da1d9895fc9cc4.svg)\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t = t_0 = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x(0) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n![](images/22/22f92a117def6f3377a72f871d58d23cf339290a55c0f7d297ccb797b5046bf7.svg)\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3s+3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3s+3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3s+3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n![](images/34/348882682fbf17c25c759980898770a40422158cc3592d1fa51aa73a941f5cb6.svg)" 13194139535474 17592186045443] [17592186228916 "Given the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ![](images/7b/7b64128fdaf3565ee121779a8fcd2c84db8f639ded96f0884d6ad7e728c83b1c.svg) So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{4(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3(t-1){e}^{4(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n![](images/7f/7f1779e39ecb5e2bcce580d20a85eae97be505e2ad0987fa45b5b9e22c52fbbf.svg)\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4s} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3(t-1){e}^{4(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443] [17592186228953 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,{{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186228954 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+{{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186229527 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {{0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.25$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417] [17592186229528 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1+2 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417] [17592186229656 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045417] [17592186229661 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatornam