& =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to use substitution $t=1+{x}^{2}$ to find ${u}_{1}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))+(x{e}^{3x})(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+{{{e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186229710 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(B-\\frac{1}{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $B$ as\n\n$$\nB:=(B-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,{{-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|.\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186229711 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={sec}^{3}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{{\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186231329 "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $14m$ and $-5m$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 14m - 6 - 5m \\\\[5pt] &= \\underbrace{14m - 5m}_{m \\textrm{terms}} - 6 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\underbrace{14m - 5m} - 6 \\\\[5pt] &= (14 - 5)m - 6 \\\\[5pt] &= 9m - 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045417] [17592186231330 "We have two groups of two like terms, $4m$ and $3m$, and $6n$ and $-2n$.\n\nTo collect these like terms, we combine each group into a single term, adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\underbrace{4m + 3m}_{m \\textrm{terms}}\\,+\\, \\underbrace{6n - 2n}_{n \\textrm{terms}} - 5 \\\\[5pt] &= (4+3)m + (6 - 2)n - 5 \\\\[5pt] &= 7m + 4n - 5 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045417] [17592186233202 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(2c,-d)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(6c,6d)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2c+6c}{2},\\frac{-d+6d}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8c}{2},\\frac{5d}{2}) \\\\  & =(4c,\\frac{5d}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417] [17592186233255 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(8,-3,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(12,5,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(12-8)^{2}+(5-(-3))^{2}+(-2-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417] [17592186233258 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(12w,2w,-4w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(7w,-6w,2w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(7w-12w)^{2}+(-6w-2w)^{2}+(2w-(-4w))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5w)^{2}+(-8w)^{2}+(6w)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{w}^{2}+64{w}^{2}+36{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125}\\,\\sqrt{{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=30\\sqrt{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w| & =30\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |w| & =6 \\\\ w & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned