)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48+27} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417] [17592186233459 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+28\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $28$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-8.\n$$" 13194139536832 17592186576411] [17592186233493 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between the given points ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-3,6)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{7-3}{2},\\frac{-4+6}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2},\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  & =(2,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417] [17592186233494 "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1 &=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=-3 \\\\ b &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186233551 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/41/41fde96921443be6c1efc28800e921a189fbe32d8a43667f6a85e7cf399eb745.png)\n\n