^{3}-5{x}^{2}+9x-45).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-5)^{2}$ is a factor of $f(x),$ $(x-5)$ must be also a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+9x-45).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $9$ | $-45$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $0$ | $45$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $9$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+9x-45}{x-2}={x}^{2}+9,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+34{x}^{2}-90x+225=(x-5)^{2}({x}^{2}+9).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+9$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={0}^{2}-4(1)(9)=-36<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+9$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+34{x}^{2}-90x+225=(x-5)^{2}{{({x}^{2}+9)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)={x}^{2}+9.$" 13194139533397 17592186045417] [17592186250475 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $22$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+16 & =22 \\\\ 2k & =22-16 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045417] [17592186250476 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(0)+f(3)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(5+(-2)+k)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(k+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3k+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $6$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k+9 & =6 \\\\ 3k & =6-9 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045417] [17592186253254 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/8f/8fe84e0056eaa8320b4824740a259226b1a18ff1b64ba648d8740d6cac8703fc.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)+f(5)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+3}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{3+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2+15 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417] [17592186253255 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/25/25dc419eb5711b0d990909c611a6eacb2754285fb45dc0d969207e63dc09ba12.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(-1)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{2+4}{2}\\cdot (-1-(-3))+\\frac{4+8}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+6+14 \\\\  & =26\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417] [17592186253354 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c4fd419e12879af4910e5d8364426c14342c3dadb970d8bbc43d31213a6ff947.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(6,7)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12y+36 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-12y+36 \\\\ 10y & =35 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-12x-14(\\frac{7}{2})+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{193}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(6)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=6$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(6,\\frac{7}{2}).$" 13194139533399 17592186045417] [17592186253393 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417] [17592186253401 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in [0,2),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [2,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,3].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =bh \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-2+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417] [17592186253405 "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-1,1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-4.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =2-2+4-4.5 \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417] [17592186253439 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1],$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =8-2\\pi -2 \\\\  & =6-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417] [17592186253527 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(-3s,-4s,7s)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(s,-6s,3s)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(s-(-3s))^{2}+(-6s-(-4s))^{2}+(3s-7s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4s)^{2}+(-2s)^{2}+(-4s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{s}^{2}+4{s}^{2}+16{s}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{s}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\,|s|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=54,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\,|s| & =54 \\\\ |s| & =9 \\\\ s & =\\pm 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417] [17592186259598 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-v+u+2 \\\\ u+v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$\n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(5,4).$" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259599 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+3v+2 \\\\ v-2u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,2u+3v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-5,3)$ and $(10,0).$\n\nDrawing this segment in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](images/e4/e4bea3edd3fcde575e68e36e08fc4186863b12450802eb0ee37c841975983486.png)" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259600 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3v+u \\\\ u-6v+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+3v \\\\ u-6v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$\n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(7,-17).$" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259607 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+2v-2 \\\\ v-u+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+2v \\\\ -u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-6,2)$ and $(8,1).$\n\nDrawing this segment in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/8e/8e57b4d47ef3c6ad1b1d70a97126aa865c4481ee67e16eaf4e79c09e3146b30d.png)" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259616 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2v+u+1 \\\\ u-v-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+2v \\\\ u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259618 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u-2v-5 \\\\ 10u-4v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u-2v \\\\ 10u-4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{vmatrix}=-4\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot 10=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v-4 \\\\ u+5v+7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}=5\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1=6\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+4v-2 \\\\ 3u+6v+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+4v \\\\ 3u+6v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}=6\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{2}$ only.\"" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259620 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-6v-2 \\\\ 5v-u+9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-6v \\\\ -u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}=5\\cdot 2-(-6)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259621 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8u-6v+2 \\\\ 4u-3v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8u-6v \\\\ 4u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}=(-3)\\cdot 8-(-6)\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259623 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+2v-5 \\\\ 2u+v+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259625 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1\\cdot (-9)-2\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+(-9)+20=7.\n$$" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259626 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-4)}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-7+(-1)+3=-5.\n$$" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259627 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-4)-2\\cdot (-5)}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+\\frac{5}{2}+(-2)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535476 17592186045443] [17592186259629 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nT