ame{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,\\pi ),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,\\pi )=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\pi  & \\pi  \\\\ \\pi  & -\\pi \\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,\\pi )=(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\pi )-\\pi \\cdot \\pi =0.\n$$" 13194139535479 17592186045443] [17592186284437 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}x \\\\ y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{d