(0,2q)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045417] [17592186290349 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(-9p,3q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(6p,5q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-9p+6p}{2},\\frac{3q+5q}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-3p}{2},\\frac{8q}{2}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3p}{2},4q)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045417] [17592186290350 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(4p,2q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-12p,7q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4p+(-12p)}{2},\\frac{2q+7q}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-8p}{2},\\frac{9q}{2}) \\\\  & =(-4p,\\frac{9q}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045417] [17592186290355 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+12x+14\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+14\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-22.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+12x+14) & =-({(x+6)}^{2}-22) \\\\  & =-(x+6)^{2}+22 \\\\  & =22-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186290356 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}-16x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${8}^{2}$, writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x+{8}^{2}}-\\,{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-65.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+16x+1 & =-((x-8)^{2}-65) \\\\  & =-(x-8)^{2}+65 \\\\  & =65-(x-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186290358 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{6}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+{1}^{2}}-\\,{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}) & =6({(p-1)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186290359 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{29}{8}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}-\\,{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}) & =-8({(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3-8{(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186290408 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}-\\,{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186290409 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+3\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-\\,{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186290593 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 11y+131\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${11}^{2}$, writing the constant term $131$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 11y+{11}^{2}}-\\,{11}^{2}+131\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+11)^{2}-{11}^{2}+131\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+11)^{2}+10.\n$$" 13194139536832 17592186576411] [17592186290594 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 4y+13\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2}$, writing the constant term $13$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}-\\,{4}^{2}+13\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+13\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+4)^{2}-3.\n$$" 13194139536832 17592186576411] [17592186290595 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+44\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${12}^{2}$, writing the constant term $44$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+{12}^{2}}-\\,{12}^{2}+44\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-12)^{2}-{12}^{2}+44\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-12)^{2}-100.\n$$" 13194139536832 17592186576411] [17592186290596 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+53\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $53$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}-\\,{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}+28.\n$$" 13194139536832 17592186576411] [17592186290597 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+0\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2}$, writing the constant term $0$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}-\\,{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}-25\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=5$ and $q=-25$." 13194139536832 17592186576411] [17592186292540 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+5\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}-\\,{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2$ and $q=1$." 13194139536832 17592186576411] [17592186292612 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(-1,4,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(2,6,5)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1){)}^{2}+(6-4{)}^{2}+(5-(-1){)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045417] [17592186292613 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(5,-2,-3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(2,2,-3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-5{)}^{2}+(2-(-2){)}^{2}+(-3-(-3){)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3{)}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045417] [17592186292614 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(3,1,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(5,-3,4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(5-3)^{2}+(-3-1)^{2}+(4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045417] [17592186292615 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(4,-2,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(3,-4,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(3-4{)}^{2}+(-4-(-2){)}^{2}+(2-(-2){)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1{)}^{2}+(-2{)}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045417] [17592186292718 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192.$ We know that $(x+4)$ and $(x-6)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $2$ | $-7$ | $-34$ | $56$ | $-192$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-8$ | $60$ | $-104$ | $192$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-15$ | $26$ | $-48$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192}{x+4}=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-6)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-6)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $2$ | $-15$ | $26$ | $-48$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $12$ | $-18$ | $48$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-3$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48}{x-6}=2{x}^{2}-3x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192 & =(x+4)(x-6)(2{x}^{2}-3x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-3x+8$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(2)(8)=-55<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-3x+8$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(x-6){{(2{x}^{2}-3x+8)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+8.$" 13194139533412 17592186045417] [17592186297201 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2)x+7 & =10 \\\\ 3(p+2)x+7-7 & =10-7 \\\\ \\underbrace{3(p+2)}_{a}x & =\\underbrace{3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3(p+2)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3(p+2)}{3} & =\\frac{0}{3} \\\\ p+2 & =0 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186297202 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-2)v-3 & =5 \\\\ (k-2)v-3+3 & =5+3 \\\\ \\underbrace{(k-2)}_{a}v & =\\underbrace{8}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nk-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-2 & =0 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186297203 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+6)x+2 & =7 \\\\ (m+6)x+2-2 & =7-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{(m+6)}_{a}x & =\\underbrace{5}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nm+6=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m+6 & =0 \\\\ m & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186297204 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(q-5)u+9 & =0 \\\\ (q-5)u+9-9 & =0-9 \\\\ \\underbrace{(q-5)}_{a}u & =\\underbrace{-9}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q-5 & =0 \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186297205 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ay=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3py & =5y+8 \\\\ 3py-5y & =5y+8-5y \\\\ 3py-5y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $y$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3p-5)y=8.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ay=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3p-5)}_{a}y=\\underbrace{8}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-5 & =0 \\\\ 3p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 3p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186297206 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $7z$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz+4 & =7z+1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =7z+1-7z \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz-7z+4 & =1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4-4 & =1-4 \\\\ 3qz-7z & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3q-7)z=-3.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3q-7)}_{a}z=\\underbrace{-3}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3q-7=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3q-7 & =0 \\\\ 3q-7+7 & =0+7 \\\\ 3q & =7 \\\\ \\frac{3q}{3} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ q & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186297207 "Suppose we have an equation of 