n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $z$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz-8 & =-z-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-z-6+z \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz+z-8 & =-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8+8 & =-6+8 \\\\ -3pz+z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(-3p+1)z=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(-3p+1)}_{a}z=\\underbrace{2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3p+1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3p+1 & =0 \\\\ -3p+1-1 & =0-1 \\\\ -3p & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{-3p}{-3} & =\\frac{-1}{-3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186297449 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,8+6 \\\\  & =\\,14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045417] [17592186297451 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-2+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045417] [17592186297456 "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change 