\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045417] [17592186297462 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,8].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8-0}\\cdot (-2+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+9\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi -4}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045417] [17592186299672 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-1]$, the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1]$, the three line segments for $x\\in [1,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}