\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}K=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}K & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}K & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ K & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ K & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535481 17592186045443] [17592186304790 "Let $f(x)=-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60.$ We know that $(x+3)$ and $(x-4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.  \n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:  \n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $-3$ | $4$ | $30$ | $-7$ | $60$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-39$ | $27$ | $-60$ |\n|  | $-3$ | $13$ | $-9$ | $20$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60}{x+3}=-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20,\n$$\n\nwhich means that  \n\n$$\n-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=(x+3)(-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division once more:  \n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-3$ | $13$ | $-9$ | $20$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $4$ | $-20$ |\n|  | $-3$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20}{x-4}=-3{x}^{2}+x-5,\n$$\n\nwhich means that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60 & =(x+3)(x-4)(-3{x}^{2}+x-5) \\\\  & =-(x+3)(x-4)(3{x}^{2}-x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $3{x}^{2}-x+5$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:  \n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(3)(5)=-59<0.\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $3{x}^{2}-x+5$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.  \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by  \n\n$$\n-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=-(x+3)(x-4){{(3{x}^{2}-x+5)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=3{x}^{2}-x+5.$" 13194139533417 17592186045417] [17592186304809 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_