itrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045417] [17592186314766 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-1,3].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semicircle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2\\pi  \\\\  & =8+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045417] [17592186314767 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-3),$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in (-3,3),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (3,6].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}