t }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\pi +2+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\pi -2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045417] [17592186317531 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{5}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region consists of two right triangles, so we find the total area by adding their areas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [5,9].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{5}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =6-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9-1}\\cdot (6-2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045417] [17592186317532 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{5}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [5,9].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{5}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{2}=6.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-4+6 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9-1}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045417] [17592186317533 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-(-2)}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045417] [17592186317534 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/a8/a86fbc456930e18159b1745f5a71fe82bf7075f3aa6d9fd4307a71a583a37a7a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(5,-3)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10x+6y+34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,1)$ and $B(6,1).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+(y-1)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-12x+36+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-12x+36+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ 4x+12x & =36-4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =(2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-10(2)+6y+34 \\\\  & =4+{y}^{2}-20+6y+34 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+6y+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=2y+6,\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(-3)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=-3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $(\\,2,-3\\,).$" 13194139533421 17592186045417] [17592186317535 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/e2/e283660466c42f7723d9e044bb2eefe148c468f7dae5a1b5882e2589e7e5f60a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(4,5)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-10y+41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-7,2)$ and $B(3,2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x+7)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+7)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+14x+49+(y-2)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+(y-2)^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{(y-2)^{2}}+14x+49 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-6x+9+\\cancel{(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ 14x+6x & =9-49 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =(-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(-2)-10y+41 \\\\  & =4+{y}^{2}+16-10y+41 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-10y+61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=2y-10,\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-10 & =0 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(5)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=5$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $(\\,-2,5\\,).$" 13194139533421 17592186045417] [17592186319102 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)+f(7)+f(11)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $k$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+k+6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4k+32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $96,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k+32 & =96 \\\\ 4k & =96-32 \\\\ k & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ k & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045417] [17592186319106 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/1c/1cb4c2c69c93117bd5eed39322059a3c0af84d16b293ccfc053c677ac0b6127d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2.5)+f(3)+f(3.5)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $2.5$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $25$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+16+9+4)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =54\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{decreasing}$ over the interval $[2,4],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." 13194139533422 17592186045417] [17592186319124 "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)={{6+2t}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population increase}}-{{(2+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population decrease}}=4+1.5t.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of wolves $T$ years after $2010.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(4+1.5t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+(4t+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =250+4T+\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=250+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}+4t.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045417] [17592186319125 "Let $c(t)$ be the rate of change of the charge. According to the given information,\n\n$$\nc(t)={{6+{t}^{3/2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{charge increase}}-{{(1+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{charge decrease}}=5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$\n\nLet $C(T)$ represent the charge stored $T$ hours after $2015.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nC(T)=C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(T) & =C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+(5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =75+5T+\\frac{2}{5}{T}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nC(t)=75-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045417] [17592186319154 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/a4/a42b281338ce0fc7d64677b260f6c0d8cdaa47855afe35bae0c21fde750a39d5.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(4)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)+f(7)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(7)+f(15)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+5}{2}\\cdot (4-0)+\\frac{5+6}{2}\\cdot (7-4)+\\frac{6+2}{2}\\cdot (15-7) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4+\\frac{11}{2}\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =12+\\frac{33}{2}+32 \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045417] [17592186319177 "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{{(\\,5\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 40 \\\\ -45\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 44 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443] [17592186319186 "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(\\frac{3}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -30 \\\\ -54\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -34 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443] [17592186320740 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2$. So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({z}^{2}+7z-8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+7z-8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z-8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({z}^{2}+7z-8) & =-2({(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186320743 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $3$. So, we take out a factor of $3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n3({q}^{2}-3q+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-3q+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({q}^{2}-3q+5) & =3({(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\\cdot \\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186320991 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2x}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045417] [17592186320992 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯+{{\\frac{2\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x+2{x}^{2}+⋯+{{\\frac{2n(n+1){x}^{n}}{(n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(n+1){x}^{n}}{(n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n!}{x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045417] [17592186321067 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in [0,4),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (4,6].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a lower semi-circle of radius $2$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (4,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-2\\pi +8+6 \\\\  & =14-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045417] [17592186321118 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,2)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [2,6].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}b\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045417] [17592186321119 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}b\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045417] [17592186323839 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}-\\,{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=-1$." 13194139536832 17592186576411] [17592186323842 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2}$, writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}-\\,{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-1.\n$$" 13194139536832 17592186576411] [17592186323905 "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $N:$\n\n$$\nN={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $N$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $N$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535483 17592186045443] [17592186323906 "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $L:$\n\n$$\nL={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $L$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $L$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535483 17592186045443] [17592186323922 "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{16} & =P{D}^{16}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{16}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[8]{2})^{16} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[4]{2})^{16}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 48 \\\\ 4 & 32\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 17592186045443] [17592186323925 "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{69} & =P{D}^{69}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{69}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{69} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{69}\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 17592186045443] [17592186323927 "We have been given the following diagonalization of $N.$\n\n$$\nN={{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $N=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${N}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{N}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{18} & =P{D}^{18}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{18}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[9]{2})^{18}\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 20 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-14 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 17592186045443] [17592186326740 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i}-7\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 17592186045443] [17592186326888 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 17592186045443] [17592186326903 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n