 corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,-2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=1+(6-\\pi )=7-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045417] [17592186332698 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9f570d7f04bcf5645a97a6a1d85e72f602c436ef184e841fc4c998c0d5eb8e2e.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-4,-1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [-1,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-4,-1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [-1,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+4.5=10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045417] [17592186332699 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/4d/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045417] [17592186332832 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(x-1)^{2}+4(x-1)^{3}-⋯+{{(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k+1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }k\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4(x-1)+12(x-1)^{2}-⋯+{{(k+1)(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }k\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }(k+1)(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=k+1.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045417] [17592186332883 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [0,1]$ and the piecewise linear for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045417] [17592186332891 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2$. So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{3}{2}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}) & =2((x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186332892 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x) & =6({(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186332893 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $5$. So, we take out a factor of $5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n5({x}^{2}-4x+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =5((x-2)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =5{(x-2)}^{2}+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5{(x-2)}^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186335766 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+6y=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{2t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha t+\\beta )=12t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha t+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=12t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha &=12\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =\\frac{5}{3},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186338843 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,1,-3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+t \\\\ y &=2-t \\\\ z &=3+2t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨1,2,3⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨1,-1,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ & =⟨2,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186342069 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(3a,-4b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-5a,6b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3a+(-5a)}{2},\\frac{-4b+6b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3a-5a}{2},\\frac{-4b+6b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-2a}{2},\\frac{2b}{2}) \\\\  & =(-a,b).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045417] [17592186342170 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-cirle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5-(-3)}\\cdot (\\frac{9\\pi }{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{16}+\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045417] [17592186342189 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ we need to check if the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[M\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -13 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -13 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=10 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2}+2(-1) & =10 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-2 & =10 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} & =12 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+(-4)-5(-1) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+1 & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${m}_{1},$ ${m}_{2},$ and ${m}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{1}}+(-4)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{2}}+(-1)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-1)=2.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186345006 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2\\cdot {a}_{1}+0\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443] [17592186345007 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2\\cdot {a}_{1}+0\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443] [17592186345026 "First, we reduce the matrix $U$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(U)$ is ${{u}_{1},{u}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${u}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{2}}={{(-2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-2)\\cdot 0=0.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443] [17592186345027 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{1}{2},{u}_{33}=3,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443] [17592186345029 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form  because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535484 17592186045443] [17592186345030 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:  \n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$  on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal. \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 