=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535486 17592186045443] [17592186354439 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535486 17592186045443] [17592186354440 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139535486 17592186045443] [17592186354455 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,4],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [4,6].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)(4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a