-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535486 17592186045443] [17592186354493 "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{24} & =P{D}^{24}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{24}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[12]{2})^{24} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[8]{2})^{24}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 40 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535486 17592186045443] [17592186354494 "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535486 17592186045443] [17592186354495 "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix 