-7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535486 17592186045443] [17592186354572 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n \n\n![](images/05/052b405c104f0d6bc353782c6e07bfb1e0245726d86346a545ea38aa31941711.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(1,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-4y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-2))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x+4 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-6y+9 \\\\ 6y & =-6x+10-4 \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(-x+1)^{2}-2x-4(-x+1)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x+1-2x+4x-4+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(0)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=-0+1=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(0,1).$" 13194139533434 17592186045417] [17592186354573 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/8b/8be39e6dde084154ecfd43718cb76352c69d26bd76d520177a6c41ee69a7f18e.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(2,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-4y+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-8x+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4y+20 \\\\ 8y & =8x-16 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(x-2)^{2}-4x-4\\cdot (x-2)+8 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4x+4-4x-4x+8+8 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-12x+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(3)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(3,1).$" 13194139533434 17592186045417] [17592186354636 "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{4}+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045417] [17592186354637 "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{5}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045417] [17592186357690 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-7y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and sinusoidal forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{{e}^{7t}}+B{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-\\alpha +6\\beta -7\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\beta -6\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{25}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{4}{25},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186357691 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{