n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t+4.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4.\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186362267 "The length of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is equal to the distance between $P$ and $Q.$\n\nThe distance $d$ between two points $P({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $Q({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ have coordinates ${{(3,\\,-2,\\,0)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and ${{(2,0,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =\\sqrt{(2-3)^{2}+(0-(-2))^{2}+(-2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045417] [17592186362268 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(-5,2,9)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(-1,-2,9)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(-1-(-5))^{2}+(-2-2)^{2}+(9-9)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045417] [17592186362361 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begi