xt{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535487 17592186045443] [17592186362369 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-6\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535487 17592186045443] [17592186362539 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}{{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Scalar curl of }F}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{{(3x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,dx+{{(-{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{Q}\\,dy\n$$\n\nIn part 2, we found that the scalar curl of $F$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$" 13194139533448 17592186045417] [17592186362675 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535487 17592186045443] [17592186362682 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535487 17592186045443] [17592186362750 "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $B$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $B:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {b}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {b}_{n} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $B$ must be $\\text{Col}(B),$ the column space of $B.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(B)$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ must be $\\text{Col}(B).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ad/ad1557fbd48206baf7239046ada69f60d8cd9ee4916b4f8e37741903ef7d1e9b.png)" 13194139535487 17592186045443] [17592186362770 "First, notice that ${A}^{T}$ is a $6\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nPre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ by ${A}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & {a}_{3}^{T} & {a}_{4}^{T} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{T}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{3}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{4}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{4}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}),$ the row space of $A.$" 13194139535487 17592186045443] [17592186363161 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=20 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-(-2) & =20 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =18 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186363168 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2} &=6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}(3) & =15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =24\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186363217 "Le