\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$9$** | **$12$** | **$16$** | **$20$** | **$23$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$1$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$11$** | **$14$** |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $8$ | $12$ | $14$ | $18$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 16 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 20 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 23 & 14\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535489 17592186045443] [17592186373972 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,z+2x⟩{|}_{z=y-2x} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x+(y-2x),\\,y-2x+2x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y-x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,z+2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (-2)-(y-x)\\cdot (1)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y-y+x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2y+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+xy]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2+2-1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533454 17592186045417] [17592186374931 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-3y & =g(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+2{y}_{p}^{′}-3{y}_{p} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′})}^{′}+2({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′})-3({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}) & =g(x) \\\\ {({u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & =g(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{({e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(9{e}^{-3x}-6{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x).\n$$" 13194139533454 17592186045417] [17592186375659 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=5 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =2+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2+2(5) \\\\  & =12, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-1-7{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-1-7(5)-(12) \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186375694 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+9{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-9,{u}_{12}=2,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-18.$" 13194139535489 17592186045443] [17592186375701 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=4,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=4.$" 13194139535489 17592186045443] [17592186375783 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,2]$ and the line segment for $x\\in [2,5].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417] [17592186375790 "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-2],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,1],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.5.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)(3) \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =-1.5+1.5+7.5-6 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417] [17592186375797 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in (1,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =6+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417] [17592186375979 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-1,3]$ and the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [3,5].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417] [17592186376000 "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the line segment for  $x\\in [-2,0]$,  the arc of a quarter-circle for $x\\in [0,1]$, the line segment for  $x\\in [1,3]$, and  the arc of a quarter-circle for $x\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,1].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+3+\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =2+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417] [17592186376021 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=6,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/14/145d9c16e27c67876d1ae2a00dce15b3cb7437f6a9121971fe4434e6c1fe943c.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [1,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [2,6],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [1,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [2,6],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(6)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+4=\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417] [17592186376028 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=1,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(1)={\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab9a16779fc4b2d4578df9bbb74c3df34c958bdb2bdd25d5db30910da7f1d9d6.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-4,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(1)={\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-4,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1)={\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+2=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417] [17592186376514 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{2}-2\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression ${n}^{2}-2$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}-2$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =(2a+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a+1)-2 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}+4a-1 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a-2)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-1$ (highlighted above), we have that ${n}^{2}-2=2b+1,$ which shows that ${n}^{2}-2$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is  one more than  a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, ${n}^{2}-2$ is odd." 13194139533455 17592186045417] [17592186376590 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\ng(m,n)\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,m\\text{ is even and }n\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P⇒Q,$ it suffices to prove the contrapositive $¬Q⇒¬P.$\n\n$$\n¬(m\\text{ is even and }n\\text{ is odd})⇒¬(g(m,n)\\text{ is odd})\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n¬(m\\text{ is even and }n\\text{ is odd})⇒g(m,n)\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even and $n$ is odd\", we use De Morgan's law for conjunctions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(m\\text{ is even and}n\\text{ is odd}) & =¬(m\\text{ is even}∧n\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & =¬(m\\text{ is even})∨¬(n\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & =m\\text{ is odd}∨n\\text{ is even} \\\\  & =m\\text{ is odd or }n\\text{ is even}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\nm\\text{ is odd or }n\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,g(m,n)\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nNow, we have two cases: $m$ is odd, or $n$ is even.\n\n- First, we consider the case when $m$ is odd.\n\n**Case 1**. If $m$ is odd, then $m=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nAs a result, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(m,n) & =(m-1)(n+4) \\\\  & =(2a+1-1)(n+4) \\\\  & =(2a)(n+4) \\\\  & =2{{a(n+4)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=a(n+4).$ Since $a$ and $n$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(m,n) & =2{{a(n+4)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Hence, $g(m,n)$ is even.\n\n- Now, we consider the case when $n$ is odd.\n\n**Case 2**. If $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nAs a result, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(m,n) & =(m-1)(n+4) \\\\  & =(m-1)(2a+4) \\\\  & =2{{(m-1)(a+2)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=(m-1)(a+2).$ Since $a$ and $m$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(m,n) & =2{{(m-1)(a+2)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Hence, $g(m,n)$ is even.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, combining the cases above, we conclude that $g(m,n)$ is odd only if $m$ is even and $n$ is odd." 13194139533455 17592186045417] [17592186377070 "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-2],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-2,1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 