{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 12 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -12{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+4(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3(0)-8(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4 & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}=2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186424810 "Here, ${53}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $53$ and the exponent is $4.$ \n\nThis means that $53$ is multiplied by itself $4$ times:\n\n$$\n{{53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{53}^{4}=53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53.\n$$" 13194139534537 17592186045417] [17592186424811 "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(7.2)}^{2} & ={{7.2\\times 7.2}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $2$ factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $7.2.$\nTherefore, the exponent $(7.2)^{2}$ will have $1\\times 2=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $5184$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n51\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{84}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(7.2)^{2}=51.84.$" 13194139534537 17592186045417] [17592186424812 "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,5)\\times (25\\,÷\\,8).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,5$ and $÷\\,8:$\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,8)\\times (25\\,÷\\,5)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{8}\\times \\frac{25}{5}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{32}{8}}_{⏟}}_{4}\\times {{\\frac{25}{5}}_{⏟}}_{5}=4\\times 5=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534537 17592186045417] [17592186424826 "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,8)\\times (24\\,÷\\,5).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,8$ and $÷\\,5:$\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,5)\\times (24\\,÷\\,8)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}\\times \\frac{24}{8}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{15}{5}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{24}{8}}_{⏟}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534537 17592186045417] [17592186426694 "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as:\n\n$$\n5÷8\\times 16\n$$\n\nWe can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷8$ and the $\\times 16:$\n\n$$\n5\\times 16÷8\n$$\n\nExpressing the division as a fraction, we arrive at:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\frac{16}{8}\n$$\n\nWe now solve:\n\n$$\n5\\times {{\\frac{16}{8}}_{⏟}}_{2}=5\\times 2=10\n$$" 13194139534586 17592186045417] [17592186426695 "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as:\n\n$$\n4÷9\\times 54\n$$\n\nWe can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷9$ and the $\\times 54:$\n\n$$\n4\\times 54÷9\n$$\n\nExpressing the division as a fraction, we arrive at:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\frac{54}{9}\n$$\n\nWe now solve:\n\n$$\n4\\times {{\\frac{54}{9}}_{⏟}}_{6}=4\\times 6=24\n$$" 13194139534586 17592186045417] [17592186426696 "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as:\n\n$$\n2÷3\\times 24\n$$\n\nWe can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷3$ and the $\\times 24:$\n\n$$\n2\\times 24÷3\n$$\n\nExpressing the division as a fraction, we arrive at:\n\n$$\n2\\times \\frac{24}{3}\n$$\n\nWe now solve:\n\n$$\n2\\times {{\\frac{24}{3}}_{⏟}}_{8}=2\\times 8=16\n$$" 13194139534586 17592186045417] [17592186426700 "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as:\n\n$$\n7÷3\\times 15\n$$\n\nWe can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷3$ and the $\\times 15:$\n\n$$\n7\\times 15÷3\n$$\n\nExpressing the division as a fraction, we arrive at:\n\n$$\n7\\times \\frac{15}{3}\n$$\n\nWe now solve:\n\n$$\n7\\times {{\\frac{15}{3}}_{⏟}}_{5}=7\\times 5=35\n$$" 13194139534586 17592186045417] [17592186426839 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:  \n\n- **Part 1:** $n$ is less than $10$ or $13.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is less than }10}_{⏟}}_{X}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{n\\text{ is less than }13}_{⏟}}_{Y},\n$$\n\nthat is, $X∨Y.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $n$ is an integer. This corresponds to $Z.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is less than }10\\text{ or }13}_{⏟}}_{X∨Y}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{n\\text{ is an integer}}_{⏟}}_{Z},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(X∨Y)∧Z.$" 13194139534591 17592186045417] [17592186427560 "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 17\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186427863 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}40 & -2 \\\\ 35 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535495 17592186045443] [17592186427864 "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535495 17592186045443] [17592186429769 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$" 13194139535495 17592186045443] [17592186429890 "Here, ${1}^{7}$ is in exponent form. The base is $1$ and the exponent is $7.$ \n\nThis means that $1$ is taken as a factor $7$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{1}^{7}={{1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1}_{⏟}}_{7\\text{ times}}=1.\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429892 "We need to evaluate ${7}^{3}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${7}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{7}^{3}={{7\\times 7\\times 7}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $7\\times 7=49,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{7\\times 7}_{⏟}}_{49}\\times 7=49\\times 7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $49\\times 7.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{6}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $49\\times 7=343,$ we have ${7}^{3}=343.$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429894 "Here, ${1}^{12}$ is in exponent form. The base is $1$ and the exponent is $12.$ \n\nThis means that $1$ is taken as a factor $12$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{1}^{12}={{1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1}_{⏟}}_{12\\text{ times}}=1.\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429896 "The square of $3$ is the same as ${3}^{2}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${3}^{2},$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}={{3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429897 "Here, $9$ raised to the power of $3$ means ${9}^{3}.$\n\nTo compute this power, we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{9}^{3}={{9\\times 9\\times 9}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $9\\times 9=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{9\\times 9}_{⏟}}_{81}\\times 9 & =81\\times 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $81\\times 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $81\\times 9=729,$ then ${9}^{3}=729.$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429899 "The square of $5$ is the same as ${5}^{2}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${5}^{2},$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2}={{5\\times 5}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}=25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429901 "We need to evaluate ${2}^{2}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${2}^{2},$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}={{2\\times 2}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429902 "We need to evaluate ${5}^{2}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${5}^{2},$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2}={{5\\times 5}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}=25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429903 "Here, ${0}^{3}$ is in exponent form. The base is $0$ and the exponent is $3.$ \n\nThis means that $0$ is taken as a factor $3$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{0}^{3}={{0\\times 0\\times 0}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{ times}}=0.\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429904 "We need to evaluate ${10}^{3}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${10}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{10}^{3}={{10\\times 10\\times 10}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $10\\times 10=100,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{10\\times 10}_{⏟}}_{100}\\times 10 & =100\\times 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $100\\times 10=1,000,$ we have ${10}^{3}=1,000.$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429905 "Here, ${0}^{11}$ is in exponent form. The base is $0$ and the exponent is $11.$ \n\nThis means that $0$ is taken as a factor $11$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{0}^{11}={{0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0}_{⏟}}_{11\\text{ times}}=0.\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429906 "We need to evaluate ${4}^{3}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${4}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{4}^{3}={{4\\times 4\\times 4}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $4\\times 4=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{4\\times 4}_{⏟}}_{16}\\times 4 & =16\\times 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $16\\times 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{2}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16\\times 4=64,$ we have ${4}^{3}=64.$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429907 "We need to evaluate ${9}^{2}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${9}^{2},$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2}={{9\\times 9}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}=81\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417] [17592186429908 "To evaluate ${9}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{9}^{3}={{9\\times 9\\times 9}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $9\\times 9=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{9\\times 9}_{⏟}}_{81}\\times 9 & =81\\times 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $81\\times 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\