∂y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=\\frac{2y}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2y}{x} & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=(-1)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$" 13194139535495 17592186045443] [17592186430655 "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,4)\\times (16\\,÷\\,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,4$ and $÷\\,3$:\n\n \n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,3)\\times (16\\,÷\\,4)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{3}\\times \\frac{16}{4}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{15}{3}}_{⏟}}_{5}\\times {{\\frac{16}{4}}_{⏟}}_{4}=5\\times 4=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534640 17592186045417] [17592186430656 "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(35\\,÷\\,6)\\times (18\\,÷\\,7).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,6$ and $÷\\,7$:\n\n$$\n(35\\,÷\\,7)\\times (18\\,÷\\,6)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting 