045417] [17592186475977 "To work out ${(-4)}^{3},$ we multiply $-4$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-4$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3} & =\\overset{16}{\\overset{⏞}{(-4)\\cdot (-4)}}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-64\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417] [17592186476920 "First, notice that ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}$ is a $n\\times m$ matrix.\n\nNow, pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{m}$ by ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{1} & ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{2} & ⋯ & ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{m} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{T}+{B}^{T}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{m}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ ({a}^{T}+{b}^{T})_{m} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}+{B}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T}),$ the row space of $A+B.$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186477030 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+2)z-8 & =-3 \\\\ (p+2)z-8+8 & =-3+8 \\\\ \\underbrace{(p+2)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{5}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\np+2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =0 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186477031 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(r-5)w-6 & =4 \\\\ 5(r-5)w-6+6 & =4+6 \\\\ \\underbrace{5(r-5)}_{a}w & =\\underbrace{10}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5(r-5)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(r-5) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5(r-5)}{5} & =\\frac{0}{5} \\\\ r-5 & =0 \\\\ r & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186477034 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q+5)v+6 & =-4 \\\\ 2(q+5)v+6-6 & =-4-6 \\\\ \\underbrace{2(q+5)}_{a}v & =\\underbrace{-10}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(q+5)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q+5) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(q+5)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ q+5 & =0 \\\\ q & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186477035 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(p-3)z-8 & =2 \\\\ 7(p-3)z-8+8 & =2+8 \\\\ \\underbrace{7(p-3)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{10}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7(p-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(p-3) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7(p-3)}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ p-3 & =0 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186477039 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-3)w-6 & =-5 \\\\ (r-3)w-6+6 & =-5+6 \\\\ \\underbrace{(r-3)}_{a}w & =\\underbrace{1}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr-3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-3 & =0 \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186477046 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $av=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $6v$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3nv & =6v+6 \\\\ 3nv-6v & =6v+6-6v \\\\ 3nv-6v & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $v$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3n-6)v=6.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3n-6)}_{a}v=\\underbrace{6}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3n-6=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-6 & =0 \\\\ 3n-6+6 & =0+6 \\\\ 3n & =6 \\\\ \\frac{3n}{3} & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ n & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186477050 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $8w$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5nw & =8w+9 \\\\ 5nw-8w & =8w+9-8w \\\\ 5nw-8w & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(5n-8)w=9.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(5n-8)}_{a}w=\\underbrace{9}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5n-8=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-8 & =0 \\\\ 5n-8+8 & =0+8 \\\\ 5n & =8 \\\\ \\frac{5n}{5} & =\\frac{8}{5} \\\\ n & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186477060 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $8w$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2kw & =8w+6 \\\\ 2kw-8w & =8w+6-8w \\\\ 2kw-8w & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2k-8)w=6.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2k-8)}_{a}w=\\underbrace{6}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2k-8=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k-8+8 & =0+8 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ \\frac{2k}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186477062 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7px & =5x+9 \\\\ 7px-5x & =5x+9-5x \\\\ 7px-5x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(7p-5)x=9.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(7p-5)}_{a}x=\\underbrace{9}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7p-5 & =0 \\\\ 7p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 7p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{7p}{7} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186477066 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $au=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $9u$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4qu+4 & =9u-8 \\\\ 4qu-9u+4 & =9u-8-9u \\\\ 4qu-9u+4 & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4qu-9u+4 & =-8 \\\\ 4qu-9u+4-4 & =-8-4 \\\\ 4qu-9u & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $u$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(4q-9)u=-12.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(4q-9)}_{a}u=\\underbrace{-12}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4q-9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4q-9 & =0 \\\\ 4q-9+9 & =0+9 \\\\ 4q & =9 \\\\ \\frac{4q}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ q & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536396 17592186045443] [17592186478379 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186478385 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{1}{2},{u}_{33}=-2,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=1.$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186478389 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=-5,{u}_{22}=4,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-20.$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186478390 "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total number of muffins.\n\nSince each of them bought $3$ blackberry muffins and $5$ strawberry muffins, the number of muffins each of them bought is:\n\n$$\n{{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blackberry muffins}}+{{5}_{⏟}}_{\\text{strawberry muffins}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person bought this number of muffins, and there are $2$ people, we must multiply the number of muffins by $2.$ Therefore, the total number of muffins is:\n\n$$\n{{(3+5)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{2}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+5)\\times 2 & =(3+5)\\times 2 \\\\  & =8\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n8\\times 2=16\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186478391 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=12.$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186478398 "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $(1+{2}^{3}).$\n\n To evaluate $(1+{2}^{3})$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n1\\,+\\overset{8}{\\overset{⏞}{{2}^{3}}}=1+8\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n1+8=9\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n5\\times {{(1+{2}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{9}=5\\times 9\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **M**ultiplication:\n\n$$\n5\\times 9=45\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186478403 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186478404 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=-\\frac{1}{3},{u}_{33}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186478406 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186478417 "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(1+3)}_{⏟}}_{4}{}^{2}={4}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n{4}^{2}=16\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186478418 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186478421 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot [y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ and $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{4}{\\pi })}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186478422 "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $({5}^{2}-1).$\n\n To evaluate $({5}^{2}-1)$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overset{25}{\\overset{⏞}{{5}^{2}}}-1=25-1\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **S**ubtraction:\n\n$$\n25-1=24\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n{{({5}^{2}-1)}_{⏟}}_{24}\\,÷8=24÷\\,8\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **D**ivision:\n\n$$\n24÷8=3\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186478426 "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(1+5)}_{⏟}}_{6}{}^{2}+{4}^{3}={6}^{2}+{4}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overset{36}{\\overset{⏞}{{6}^{2}}}+\\overset{64}{\\overset{⏞}{{4}^{3}}}=36+64\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n36+64=100\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186478435 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186478437 "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(2+3)}_{⏟}}_{5}-{2}^{2}=5-{2}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n5\\,-\\overset{4}{\\overset{⏞}{{2}^{2}}}=5\\,-4\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **S**ubtraction:\n\n$$\n5-4=1\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186478439 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(3{x}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186478458 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3b}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{2y{e}^{-3x}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(3{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417] [17592186479056 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=2 \\\\ -3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=2$ and ${y}_{2}=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186479088 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-2 \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-2$ and ${y}_{2}=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186479090 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=6 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =6, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-2+{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-(6)+4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186479095 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-4, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-5-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-5-2(-4) \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =9+4{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\  & =9+4(-4)+3(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186479106 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=8 \\\\ -8{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=8$ and ${y}_{2}=50.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 50\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186479110 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=1 \\\\ 9{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=1$ and ${y}_{2}=-3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186479113 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=3 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=3$ and ${y}_{2}=-12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-12.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-12$ and ${x}_{1}=-5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186479139 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=3 \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =1-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =1-(3) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-3-2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-3-2(3)+2(-2) \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186479145 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 11 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 50\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=9 \\\\ 11{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=50.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=9$ and ${y}_{2}=-49.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -49\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535661 17592186045443] [17592186479848 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t)+3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t)+3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186479854 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186481341 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,6,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/00/0036b6bd64900e2c6a0319f16788b2d563a8415256e2f21ba561b1fb41afdf34.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ and top-left corner at $(6,6),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/43/4394f404e54b8e585becb601f688eece3d4769efe2a124cb53b11d3432a9996b.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,6)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/5e/5ed86031d5762e3a2fe440fba52775fc6833243664fa18ee85c3449b50ff6cee.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6,Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(6,6) \\\\  & =0.7-0.4 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417] [17592186481349 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/01/01e04dfdac5f4abc16bd856dcfbe1b7361010cce2e7779658a33f293c1c52ae4.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2d2525118ca3b62c0829c0308fab168d0b806dfa13302c007a63cd354314d63f.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(4,-1)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,-1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 4,-1<Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le -1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,1)-F(4,-1)-F(1,1)+F(1,-1) \\\\  & =0.77-0.15-0.19+0.09 \\\\  & =0.52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417] [17592186481372 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6\\}\\times \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/2b/2b159f4ba1873270f42b7afd2fdc4b969672c7d3cb7e694d519ab82dab33b283.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/e1/e186cf0e38e938798cdcd5bc10a5334230cc78cc77d114a14eba0c07702a1cdc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,3)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/eb/ebcfd8e0b553cab38a76eac77aab45186db92b3fe47a1fbb87a8623db4c29389.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4,1<Y\\le 3) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 3)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,3)-F(4,1) \\\\  & =0.8-0.3 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417] [17592186481393 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2dd2929b39c9d0357516ebea9f1f31ed2b5f64c6154184ec904996c51162c144.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(5,3)$ and bottom-left corner at $(2,1)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/db/db601c0b0d2931a61d2218e196ea7485fc8d258cb77c604dc44661687de43beb.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,3).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(2,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 5,2\\le Y<4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,3)-F(5,1)-F(2,3)+F(2,1) \\\\  & =0.6-0.23-0.32+0.05 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417] [17592186481396 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}\\times \\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/f4/f43c5ae8d27872c4aebe235c507600b7bf1806e22b52dd3b3ff2261509ac9925.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(10,9)$ and bottom-left corner at $(4,3)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/60/60bf5a958b682d513010c3eff9a8084072be62e286c0b0dfacb09960a3283e38.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(10,3)$ and $(4,9).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,3).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<11,4<Y<10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 3)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,3)-F(4,9)+F(4,3) \\\\  & =0.55-0.15-0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417] [17592186481406 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/dd/dd49cc4b7cb5cc80bb325819c5c8f3c31f8bc3ed762139c2c3b9567a24db2324.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/34/344fec686907843b4b9375db138592f93be9d0232500fb35dceb90086db503cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,1)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,-1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/6e/6e3a7389008d67f4096fdaa6a98c0e3e2bdeda64ec1e47b93a6d3ba66a54b18d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,-1<Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -1)}} \\\\  & =F(1,1)-F(1,-1) \\\\  & =0.6-0.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417] [17592186481414 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186481420 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/ae/ae7d480e52257825e679349ce185b4aa08ed3b096fd7a780ece56c90439798fe.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(1,1)$ and bottom-left corner at $(-2,-2)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/5b/5bcb1b0b518485dfcd4066a0259fea4a2dc050be47e95b4de0b925d8f1c997aa.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(1,-2)$ and $(-2,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,-2).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.5\\le X\\le 1.5,-1.5\\le Y\\le 1.5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le -2)}} \\\\  & =F(1,1)-F(1,-2)-F(-2,1)+F(-2,-2) \\\\  & =0.7-0.3-0.4+0.1 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417] [17592186481437 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/71/715408bbd41d8551b8eadce9df6259f763ec34a702d48f4d116c4164ae5fda50.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2c/2c4dbaa771e897551b72f0c12ad2ba6940dfbef450c89815dc0f8912babd6a05.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,6)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/fb/fb0624aa6e88d4811e77d3c38f24d1d20d201b8e7f6cfb23a3278afef794ab5d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 12,Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(6,6) \\\\  & =0.6-0.12 \\\\  & =0.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417] [17592186481438 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/cd/cdb5c8084958ba3dc1175c1e92035d89fb4edcdf8ce333962b49f22b40552f71.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(5,1)$ and top-left corner at $(3,1),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/1c/1c28f3e2f820511a30aa64ccd3affacaa6d72e69da9c596827429b244bf1129a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,1)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/ae/ae993ae6e8c0d71a42ca0e11ed5b30a8d762b3fab12771247eca262f0131c4f5.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3,Y<2) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,1)-F(3,1) \\\\  & =0.67-0.43 \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417] [17592186482514 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186482531 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535497 17592186045443] [17592186482549 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}10