)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}3 v^{3}&9 u v^{2}\\\\2 u&4 v\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(3 v^{3})\\cdot (4 v)-(9 u v^{2})\\cdot (2 u)\\\\& =- 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 12 v^{4}\\\\& =- 6 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(3,1)} & =\\left(- 6 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=3,v=1}\\\\& =- 6 (1)^{2} \\left(- 2 (1)^{2} + 3 (3)^{2}\\right)\\\\& =-150\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-150|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =150\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535581 17592186045443] [17592186518966 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =\\left|\\begin{matrix}8 \\left(2 u + v\\right)&4 \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}&- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\& =(8 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})-(4 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})\\\\& =- 72 u^{3} + 108 u^{2} v - 36 v^{3}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(1,-1)} & =\\left(- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=1,v=-1}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(- (-1) + (1)\\right)^{2} \\left((-1) + 2 (1)\\right)\\\\& =-144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-144|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =144\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535581 17592186045443] [17592186519537 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/71/715408bbd41d8551b8eadce9df6259f763ec34a702d48f4d116c4164ae5fda50.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/5c/5c50878b85d26a87d6220a99f6fbe896925a6802801117239a5ba56dcba46906.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(12,4)$ and $(6,6).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,4).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 12,4<Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(12,4)-F(6,6)+F(6,4) \\\\  & =0.81-0.3-0.17+0.1 \\\\  & =0.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417] [17592186519541 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/9e/9e7509e29912cc54dde9a86786f1d171a49b89e7b5f7305ef025582b3402350a.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/78/78a130ca07be1acecc4655a557abb8d3d037da28f143d0696272d12898b6b4cd.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,10)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/e4/e4ff1e7cd8259fa9bcaae75210a7a3f2aeeb547ceba46b2fb411f94133372fa2.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7,4<Y\\le 10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 10)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(7,10)-F(7,4) \\\\  & =0.82-0.11 \\\\  & =0.71.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417] [17592186519599 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/a7/a782578ef55726c98ed23d89cf3de531ede60fe5db3fa9062c05507c5c2f60af.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ and top-left corner at $(3,4),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/7b/7ba99957a743b5cf283c2668e81b6c1aeb06730d47fc157aec90fe8bc880ced4.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/3b/3bd652b8d918e26cd330ad43555f039845d0b5405b70c96828bb2d652b7f872c.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(12,4)-F(3,4) \\\\  & =0.6-0.15 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417] [17592186520486 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{y}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x+{e}^{y}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{x{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x+1y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-1)xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-1)=1.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417] [17592186520510 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot x+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{32})xy+(-\\frac{1}{64}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{32})+(-\\frac{1}{64})=\\frac{13}{64}.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417] [17592186521467 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{16}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}) & =-8({(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186521469 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{2}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-x\\cdot (-2y)+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f2f4cb03b3b0232c9debd1f4ed64bd683fe788ccba16cfbe395d9c3b629972d.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417] [17592186521522 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+z+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({z}^{2}+z+2) & =-({(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186521557 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2$. So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x-2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x-2) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186521565 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}+3y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+3y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-3y-5 & =-({(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411] [17592186523930 "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates ${{(-2,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(2,7)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-2))^{2}+(7-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417] [17592186523944 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/13/137e3d2e3344f5d1f57f36d470564a518c359bb46d5278382b7242e73999e80e.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,4).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(4,4).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,4)-F(5,1)-F(4,4)+F(4,1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{3}{9}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417] [17592186523947 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/03/0378964a9f167554e4faeba228ebae485de94dda83fbd6704a864a06b8abe77a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,5)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/9079baa6464df773cacb820a6bb4a8e2b8f4ecc19755619a9b5c191a0c48507f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 5)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & =F(1,5)-F(-2,5) \\\\  & =0.63-0.27 \\\\  & =0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417] [17592186523992 "In this case, we have ${{(-4\\sqrt{5},-2\\sqrt{5})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(\\sqrt{5},-12\\sqrt{5})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{5}-(-4\\sqrt{5}))}^{2}+{(-12\\sqrt{5}-(-2\\sqrt{5}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(-10\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25(5)+100(5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125+500} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{625} \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417] [17592186524026 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/d3/d31cdb5e3f201d9dd5b3b622d883f67bb2c8bc0a5036905695a5cc5ceb3bda07.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,8)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/62/626d5c4cba7b41d6b6f2da77ca1d62c5bcd442990ce31cf630e69882dd6580b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,8)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 8)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(8,8)-F(8,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417] [17592186524958 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot 